  "response": "Here's a Python function called `reverse_list_elements(elements, punctuation)` that reverses the order of items in both the `elements` and `punctuation` lists while preserving their relative associations:\n\n```python\ndef reverse_list_elements(elements, punctuation):\n    \"\"\"\n    Reverses the order of elements and their corresponding punctuation marks.\n\n    Args:\n        elements (list): List of string elements to be reversed.\n        punctuation (list): List of punctuation marks corresponding to each element.\n\n    Returns:\n        tuple: A tuple containing two lists — (reversed_elements, reversed_punctuation).\n\n    Example:\n        >>> reverse_list_elements([\"Hello\", \"world\"], [\"\", \"!\"])\n        ([\"world\", \"Hello\"], [\"!\", \"\"])\n    \"\"\"\n    new_elements = elements[::-1]\n    new_punctuation = punctuation[::-1]\n    return (new_elements, new_punctuation)\n```\n\n### Example Usage:\n```python\nelements = [\"Hello\", \"world\"]\npunctuation = [\"\", \"!\"]\n\nresult = reverse_list_elements(elements, punctuation)\nprint(result)  # Output: (['world', 'Hello'], ['!', ''])\n```\n\n### Additional Test Cases:\n```python\n# Empty lists\nprint(reverse_list_elements([], []))  \n# Output: ([], [])\n\n# Different lengths (preserves structure of each list independently)\nprint(reverse_list_elements([\"A\", \"B\"], [\"!\", \"?\"]))\n# Output: (['B', 'A'], ['?', '!'])\n```\n\n### Notes:\n- If `elements` and `punctuation` differ in length, each is reversed independently.\n- This preserves the relative mapping between original elements and punctuation indices post-reversal.",
